From 836d513333612e876732ca26d44049aab1a8daef Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: clsx524 <clsx524@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 23:02:37 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] docs: describe the aarch64-caihong platform

Record the board's platform contract for agents and contributors.
---
 AGENTS.md | 3 ++-
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/AGENTS.md b/AGENTS.md
index c67aeb1e9..882c24ca8 100644
--- a/AGENTS.md
+++ b/AGENTS.md
@@ -73,6 +73,7 @@ Omarchy runs on x86 and aarch64 (ARM). A platform name is the CPU architecture,
 - `aarch64` - every ARM machine without a family of its own (Raspberry Pis and ARM VMs today)
 - `aarch64-apple` - Apple Silicon Macs
 - `aarch64-qualcomm` - Snapdragon laptops
+- `aarch64-caihong` - OnePlus Pad 2/Pad Pro tablets running Caihong Linux
 - `aarch64-n1x` - NVIDIA N1x laptops
 - `aarch64-gb10` - NVIDIA GB10 desktops (DGX Spark, ASUS Ascent GX10)
 
@@ -81,7 +82,7 @@ A new family is named `aarch64-<family>` (e.g. `aarch64-qualcomm`, `aarch64-n1x`
 Ask a helper; don't read `uname -m` or the device tree in feature code:
 
 - `omarchy-hw-x86` / `omarchy-hw-aarch64` - the CPU architecture. Use them for binary and ABI availability: a package with no aarch64 build, an x86 guest VM, multilib.
-- `omarchy-hw-aarch64-apple`, `omarchy-hw-aarch64-qualcomm`, `omarchy-hw-aarch64-n1x`, `omarchy-hw-aarch64-gb10` (one per family) - built on `omarchy-hw-platform`, image-build aware. Use them for hardware behaviour that the whole family shares; a single model's quirks stay behind `omarchy-hw-match` on its DMI name, or behind `omarchy-hw-match-device-tree` on its device-tree compatible string where models share a DMI name. `omarchy-hw-apple-silicon` and `omarchy-hw-n1x` are hidden aliases kept for the omarchy-mac packages and older N1x images; don't use them in new code.
+- `omarchy-hw-aarch64-apple`, `omarchy-hw-aarch64-qualcomm`, `omarchy-hw-aarch64-caihong`, `omarchy-hw-aarch64-n1x`, `omarchy-hw-aarch64-gb10` (one per family) - built on `omarchy-hw-platform`, image-build aware. Use them for hardware behaviour that the whole family shares; a single model's quirks stay behind `omarchy-hw-match` on its DMI name, or behind `omarchy-hw-match-device-tree` on its device-tree compatible string where models share a DMI name. `omarchy-hw-apple-silicon` and `omarchy-hw-n1x` are hidden aliases kept for the omarchy-mac packages and older N1x images; don't use them in new code.
 
 Where platform code lives:
 
